{"chunks": [{"id": "E:\\vsprojects\\BitNet\\webui\\data\\uploads\\L01_Annotated_Reading_1.docx:0:40c6d0d1865f", "source": "E:\\vsprojects\\BitNet\\webui\\data\\uploads\\L01_Annotated_Reading_1.docx", "text": "Annotated Reading Stephen Costigan ENG 1 0 1 11 th September 2017 How to Mark a Book Owning a book is more than purchasing one, but then what is it to truly own a book? Alder (1) suggests that one does not truly own a book until they have made the book \u201ca part of [themselves].\u201d His intention in writing was not simply one of random notation, but to absorb a book for its content and produce margin notes to keep track of ideas and questions (3) among other things. The words of his article were ones any who read his article could relate to, but perhaps had not formed into cognitive thought. The idea that ownership of a book is more than an act of possession, but one of intense effort and dedication, that a book is not truly yours until they are \u201c are as much a part of you as your head or your heart\u201d (4). Works Cited Alder, Mortimer J. How to Mark a Book . The Saturday Review of Literature, July 6, 1941", "tokens": ["annotated", "reading", "stephen", "costigan", "eng", "1", "0", "1", "11", "th", "september", "2017", "how", "to", "mark", "a", "book", "owning", "a", "book", "is", "more", "than", "purchasing", "one", "but", "then", "what", "is", "it", "to", "truly", "own", "a", "book", "alder", "1", "suggests", "that", "one", "does", "not", "truly", "own", "a", "book", "until", "they", "have", "made", "the", "book", "a", "part", "of", "themselves", "his", "intention", "in", "writing", "was", "not", "simply", "one", "of", "random", "notation", "but", "to", "absorb", "a", "book", "for", "its", "content", "and", "produce", "margin", "notes", "to", "keep", "track", "of", "ideas", "and", "questions", "3", "among", "other", "things", "the", "words", "of", "his", "article", "were", "ones", "any", "who", "read", "his", "article", "could", "relate", "to", "b